Subject: Particle filters - settinig data conditional on model's estimates
Message-ID: <UmxNG6MAxVk.jUDWbslXBwAJ@nimble-users.groups.google.com>
Archived-At: <https://groups.google.com/g/nimble-users/c/UmxNG6MAxVk/m/jUDWbslXBwAJ>
MIME-Version: 1.0
Content-Type: text/plain; charset="utf-8"
Content-Transfer-Encoding: 8bit

Dear developers & users.

I'm trying to use a simple particle filter for a toy model of human behavior. Assume the following very simple model:

y[t]~dnorm(x[t],sensoryNoise)
x[t]~dnorm(x[t-1]+c[t],processNoise)
c[t]<-w(0-x[t-1])

Thus, c[t] models human action with the goal of reaching 0 (where the real x values start from 100, for example), and w is some constant defining objective limits to finishing tasks (tasks take time, and multiple steps...). However, behavior (c) should influence not only the estimated x, but also the actual state in the real world. In all particle filter examples, real-world states and observations are generated in R and passed to nimble as data. The question is whether there is any option of doing this within the model (for example, can I use rnorm within the model?)

Thanks,
Isaac.

From udd632bd3bbcdd7e9 at groups.google.com  Mon Jul 16 06:41:23 2018
From: udd632bd3bbcdd7e9 at groups.google.com (Perry de Valpine)
Date: Mon, 16 Jul 2018 06:41:23 +0000
Subject: Re: Particle filters - settinig data conditional on model's estimates
Message-ID: <UmxNG6MAxVk.9YGKbI9lAwAJ@nimble-users.groups.google.com>
In-Reply-To: <UmxNG6MAxVk.jUDWbslXBwAJ@nimble-users.groups.google.com>
References: <UmxNG6MAxVk.jUDWbslXBwAJ@nimble-users.groups.google.com>
Archived-At: <https://groups.google.com/g/nimble-users/c/UmxNG6MAxVk/m/9YGKbI9lAwAJ>
MIME-Version: 1.0
Content-Type: text/plain; charset="utf-8"
Content-Transfer-Encoding: 8bit

Hi Itzik,

Thanks for the question.

I don't completely follow your question, but let me try to explain what nimble should be be doing.  The observations are passed as data, but the (unknown) states are simulated by the particle filter.  For example, in the first example in section 8.1.1, in the nimbleModel call, y is given as data, but not x.  In the call to buildBoostrapFilter, the unknown states are indicated to be in variable "x".  This means that the "x[i]" will be simulated.

Even though a model declaration says "x[i] ~ dnorm(x[i-1] * a + b, 1)", the "rnorm" function will be used if this node is simulated by an algorithm.  The declaration means "x[i] follows this distribution", and then whichever function (dnorm or rnorm) is needed will be used (depending on whether the algorithm code has "calculate" or "simulate", respectively).

In your model, if the states are "x", then the particle filter will determine how to simulate from x[t-1] to x[t] using the graphical model declared by your code.  It looks like c[t] depends on x[t-1], and x[t] depends on c[t] and x[t-1].  The algorithm will use these in correct (topologically sorted) order, in this case c[t] before x[t].

One way to check that the steps are being called as you expect would be to run the particle filter uncompiled and use R's debugging to see when functions are called.  You can include "browser()" statements in the w nimbleFunction (which presumably you wrote), and you could do "debug(rnorm)" to catch rnorm being called.

Hope this helps.  Let us know further questions.  Often we can be more helpful if you include a full reproducible example.

-Perry

